Z||000000||MR-BAD||07-02-2001||08:44 PM||||i was just wondering how well does a fiero 2m4 automatic handle in the winter?<P>||||reg||10
Z||000001||DecadenceR||07-02-2001||08:51 PM||||Only problem I ever had was when I was going 40 and tried to slow down too quick to turn into a drive way...<BR>I overshot like 5 feet or so. but otherwise it doesn't handle bad at all. Just gotta keep an eye on your speed.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||07-02-2001||09:19 PM||||I spent part of last winter in a 2m4 automatic.  Corners are easy.  Just get yourself started into a corner, then mash on the gas.  You'll end up going in the right direction.  Just remember to steer and counter steer.  What a blast!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||07-02-2001||11:19 PM||||what MM said.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I drove my 84 2m4 auto all winter 2 years ago... last winter I drove my 86 GT 5 speed.  The 84 was much easier, and more fun.  They're surprisingly good in the snow, if you have decent tires.  Even made it home through a blizzard with about a foot of snow on the ground, that front air dam makes a nice little snowplow  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/wacko.gif">  <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed (soon!!)<BR>1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<P>AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrgone||07-03-2001||12:46 AM|||| man i cant imagine driving my fiero in the snow, i gotta practice recovering from fishtails in the rain some more.<BR>cause ill have to snow it sooner or later<P>btw rain is fun||||reg||1
Z||000005||DecadenceR||07-03-2001||12:57 AM||||I just wish the hand brake was easier to access. It woudl be SO much more fun than my Saturn was to do douhnuts in parking lots...||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||07-03-2001||02:26 AM||||With snow tires or good all season tires you should be fine. That's assuming the snows not too deep. Ground clearance can be a problem if you have to go somewhere unplowed after a big snow. It's really only an inch or two less than most small cars though so it's really not THAT much of a difference. It handles different than a FWD, not really worse, just different. The only other problem is trying to see over snow banks at intersections...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||MR-BAD||07-03-2001||08:12 PM||||i see||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tweek||07-03-2001||09:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B>With snow tires or good all season tires you should be fine. That's assuming the snows not too deep. Ground clearance can be a problem if you have to go somewhere unplowed after a big snow. It's really only an inch or two less than most small cars though so it's really not THAT much of a difference. It handles different than a FWD, not really worse, just different. The only other problem is trying to see over snow banks at intersections...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My Boss's '00 Grand Ma (mis-spelled on purpose) got stuck on the way out of our unplowed praking lot at work (about 6-7 in.) But I drove circles around him, easy to do with cheap skinny big knobed snow tires on my '84 auto||||reg||1
Z||000009||Steve Normington||07-04-2001||02:03 AM||||Winter driving means you don't have to run the AC (see location).||||reg||1
Z||000010||poupas||07-04-2001||04:09 PM||||Fieros in winter are excellent. I drove both a 4 and a 6cyl. in the winter and the handle great. All the weight is in the rear, perfect for rear wheel drive. Just make sure the tires are good. We get alot of snow up here.||||reg||1
Z||000011||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-04-2001||04:17 PM||||drove mine 2 winters out of the 10 years I had her.. one winter as a standard, one as an automatic.. It handles great as long as you got good tires.. I would also recommend weighing down the front trunk with at least 50 pounds to give extra steering weight.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||KnightRyder31||07-04-2001||04:40 PM||||It's very easy.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||07-04-2001||05:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DecadenceR:<BR><B>I just wish the hand brake was easier to access. It woudl be SO much more fun than my Saturn was to do douhnuts in parking lots...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hand brake??  Donuts??  Not even!  I did donuts in the parking lot of the factory I worked at winter before last.  It's easy.  Just crank the wheel and mash on the gas.  I got 14 or 15 one morning before my boss noticed and put a quick stop to it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  What a blast!  I wouldn't even consider doing that in 4Play, though.  What if I hit something?!<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000014||Uaana||07-05-2001||01:21 AM||||Only time I've ever had a problem in winter was when I had a high idle problem on my GT.. thing had a tendency to understeer in corners.<BR>Other than that they get better traction than most 2 wheel drive pickups as long as you have decent tires.. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||07-05-2001||01:53 AM||||You have to watch icy corners... Tap the gas too hard, and your rear bumper comes up to say hi to you... Other than that, nice and predictable.||||reg||6
